## Lore

An Administrator is one of the Level 5 citizens who run Melbourne or Perth:
the owners, chief executives, department heads, police commissioners and
senior officers at the top of a society where the line between government
and corporation is thin. They are wealthy, well educated and well informed,
and between them they hold most of the Tech-Cities'' money and elected
power. They know many of the secrets the ordinary citizen is never told,
about the economy and about the world outside the walls, though never all
of them. Some know enough that a Special Operations detail guards them, and
would kill them if they went rogue.

A player Administrator is the exception among his peers: a maverick who
speaks for ordinary people or fights for an issue, unpopular with the other
Level 4 and 5 citizens, slandered, and a target for ambitious rivals,
hawkish military leaders and the old power brokers who like things as they
are.

Rank has its uses. An Administrator may own mega-damage weapons, armor and
vehicles, leave the City more or less at will, requisition equipment and
staff from his own field, and put new policy on the table.

## GM Notes

The book calls this class better kept as an NPC villain or powerful ally,
and says the G.M. is within his rights to refuse it to a player. No player
character may ever belong to the City Administration Office.

Fringe benefits the sheet does not list: a limousine and driver, a personal
trainer, a secretary and similar staff (useful NPCs); 1D4 luxury cars (300
M.D.C., 200 mph/320 km, wet bar, air conditioning, stereo, two-way radio,
phone and computer link, no weapons); and a home in the upper levels of the
city with a pool, hot tub or sauna, gym and several bedrooms. Also a Level 5
Citizen Card, an expensive wristwatch and a portable phone.

Money: 2D6x5000 dollars in savings, and close to half a million more tied
up in cars, the home and investments. Salary is $15,000 +1D6x1,000 a month,
rising $300 per level, before bonuses and perks; half of it goes on rent,
personal employees and bills. Australian dollars equal credits.

Requisitions: equipment takes 1D6x10 hours to clear and personnel 1D6x5
hours, each divided by the character''s level. Personnel number 1D6 per
level, doubled if they work directly under him. Invoke Policy is 4% +2% per
level plus the character''s M.A.

An Administrator working in a medical field may take any Medical skill at
+10% instead of First Aid only.
